      "problem": "Identify the surface $-x^2 + z^2 + 2x + y=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square in the $x$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2x+y & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-2x)+{z}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)^{2}+1+{z}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by rearranging, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x-1)^{2}+1+{z}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-y-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-{z}^{2} & =y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $(1, -1, 0).$",
